<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The two determinism-critical ports' real implementation: IClockPort and
    IIdGeneratorPort over the BCL. No vendor package, nothing but System.

    shared/, not client/ or server/: both hosts need to know what time it is and both
    hosts mint identifiers, so a single project serves them rather than two copies of
    four lines drifting apart.

    It exists at all because ambient time and ambient identity are exactly what must not
    be reachable from the rules. DateTimeOffset.UtcNow and Guid.NewGuid() are banned
    outright above this layer, so a reading and a fresh id enter through a port like any
    other outside dependency — and the composition root is the only place that names this
    project.

    🔒 A1 — WHAT MAY JOIN THESE TWO, AND WHAT MAY NOT. `23` §5 A1 is "one adapter project
    per external dependency. Never a shared 'Infrastructure' project", and two ports in
    one project is only A1-compliant because they wrap ONE dependency: the host process's
    ambient state, reached through System and nothing else. That is the same licence
    `23` §3 grants Platform.Godot, which carries four ports over one runtime.

    So the bound is the dependency, not the word "ambient": an implementation belongs here
    if and only if it is a handful of lines over the BCL with no package, no configuration
    and no I/O. Ambient ENVIRONMENT — a filesystem path, an environment variable, a locale,
    a device fact — is a different dependency with a different failure mode and gets its own
    project (Cache.LocalFile and Platform.Godot already own two of those). A third port added
    here that needs a PackageReference is A1 breached and this comment is the thing that
    should have stopped it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\SlayIdleRepeat.Application\SlayIdleRepeat.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
